        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks again for taking your time and implementing this PR!\r\n\r\nHowever, after trying this out I still concur the reasoning in [this comment](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/pull/780#issuecomment-2036878883). Dynamic width makes navigation a lot less visually consistent than I'd like it to be. It might be OK with only two windows (although I still don't really like it) but it is definitely not OK with bigger number of windows.\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nCouple of suggestions in case you'd want to have this in your own fork:\r\n- I'd use `max = math.max(max, vim.fn.strdisplaywidth(v))` to compute maximum line width inside the cycle, as it gets a better width in presence of icons.\r\n- The resulting width in non-preview windows will always be a bit greater than needed due to how 'mini.files' tracks its content by [prepending each line with concealed path identifier](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/32b2db5ef9066f3d7ca375ea1ccc0fc44090727d/doc/mini-files.txt#L236-L238). This can also be taken into account with addition `string.match()`, though.",
